lesson k-sound-buttons v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=4 subject=reading levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Sound buttons"
  goal "Say the sound over each button and push the sounds together into a word."

  look {
    scene 24x9 {
      soundbuttons sb graphemes=["c", "a", "t"] count=false at=canvas(1, 1)
      art pic asset="cat" right-of=sb gap=2
    }
    say """
      Each dot is a button. Press it with a finger and say its sound: c, a, t. Now say them faster
      and faster until they run together: cat. A bar under two letters is one button, because the
      two letters make one sound.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.blend-to-animal k=0 p=2 level=medium
    }
    practice k.blend-to-animal count=2 seed=7 easy-count=1
    practice k.two-sound-word count=2 seed=3
    practice k.missing-middle count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    practice k.last-button count=2 seed=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.letters-or-sounds k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.make-an-animal k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Press each button and say its sound. Then push the sounds together."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Blending is hard at first, and many children say the sounds correctly and still cannot hear the
    word. Say the sounds with them, closer and closer together, and let the child finish the word.
    The middle sound is the hardest one to hear, so give it time. If the child is stuck, use robot
    talk through the day: can you find your c, u, p? Off the page, sound out the names of things on
    the table and let the child blend them. England, Reception, ELG Word Reading: "Read words
    consistent with their phonic knowledge by sound-blending".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the animals are the cat and the dog, one blend is worked in the teacher's pen first, and only one middle sound is asked."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the blends are hen, pig and fox, the two-letter sounds are in fish and chin, and the three-star's cards make pig or hen."
  }
}
